The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (!atomic_add_unless(&i2c_priv->tfm_count, 1, 1))
    return 0;
if (!work_data)
    return -ENOMEM;
Reason:
The function atomically increments `tfm_count` before attempting memory allocation. If the allocation fails, the function returns -ENOMEM without decrementing `tfm_count`. This leaves the counter incremented permanently, causing subsequent reads to be blocked because they check `tfm_count` (via `atomic_add_unless`).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!work_data) {
    atomic_dec(&i2c_priv->tfm_count);
    return -ENOMEM;
}
Reason:
When memory allocation fails, the fix decrements `tfm_count` before returning -ENOMEM. This correctly undoes the earlier increment, ensuring the counter remains accurate and future read operations are not permanently blocked.

